  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nAn extended period of relatively quiet weather begins across the \nforecast area starting today courtesy of high pressure. The broad \nscale synoptic pattern will see an upper ridge that is currently \npositioned across the desert southwest, gradually shift eastward \nthroughout the weekend and early/mid next week, eventually ending up \nacross the Tennessee Valley. This translates into a lengthy period \nof mainly dry weather across the forecast area amid a steady warming \ntrend. \n\nA few isolated showers do remain possible this afternoon and \nevening via terrain enhanced lift, with one or two ongoing at \npresent across the northeast CWA. This also remains possible on \nSaturday afternoon/evening. A return to a somewhat more active \nperiod in terms of showers/storms could occur middle to end of \nnext work week when the ridge is progged to flatten/shift by \nsome guidance.\n\nHigh temperatures today in the low/mid 80s across the lowlands will \ntransition to upper 80s to low 90s by Sunday as a late summer heat \nwave commences, then remain as such through much of the next work \nweek, with even mid 90s possible in some locations on \nTuesday/Wednesday. Mainly clear skies will result in plenty of \nsunshine during the day, with the potential for valley fog at night, \ndense in some locations. Tonight will be the coolest night of the \nperiod with lows in the 50s to low 60s across much of the area. \nThereafter, mid 60s to low 70s are generally expected for overnight \nlows across the lowlands through much of next week.",
